// U1 L5 Types of Forces — 25-question multiple-choice bank
::PHY-U1-L5-Q01::A stretched spring pulls toward equilibrium because the spring force is {=opposite the displacement ~in the displacement direction ~always upward ~always equal to weight #### The negative sign in F=-kx indicates the restoring direction.}
::PHY-U1-L5-Q02::The SI unit of spring constant k is {=N/m ~N·m ~kg/m ~m/N #### From F=kx, k has units newtons per meter.}
::PHY-U1-L5-Q03::A spring with k=200 N/m is stretched 0.10 m. What is the force magnitude? {=20 N ~2 N ~2000 N ~0.0005 N #### F=kx=(200)(0.10)=20 N.}
::PHY-U1-L5-Q04::If the stretch of an ideal spring doubles, its spring-force magnitude {=doubles ~halves ~quadruples ~does not change #### Hooke's law gives direct proportionality.}
::PHY-U1-L5-Q05::Tension on an object acts {=along the rope or cable away from the object ~perpendicular to the rope ~opposite gravity in every case ~only when the object moves #### Tension follows the taut connector.}
::PHY-U1-L5-Q06::The normal force exerted by a surface is directed {=perpendicular to the surface ~parallel to the surface ~always upward ~toward Earth's center #### Normal means perpendicular.}
::PHY-U1-L5-Q07::Static friction differs from kinetic friction because static friction {=adjusts up to a maximum before sliding begins ~always equals μkFN ~acts only after sliding begins ~never changes magnitude #### Static friction matches the needed opposing force up to its limit.}
::PHY-U1-L5-Q08::A 10 kg box rests on a level floor with no other vertical forces. Using g=9.8 m/s², the normal force is {=98 N ~10 N ~9.8 N ~980 N #### Vertical equilibrium gives FN=mg=98 N.}
::PHY-U1-L5-Q09::A 98 N normal force and μk=0.20 produce kinetic friction of {=19.6 N ~4.9 N ~98.2 N ~490 N #### fk=μkFN=(0.20)(98)=19.6 N.}
::PHY-U1-L5-Q10::The coefficient of friction is {=dimensionless ~measured in newtons ~measured in kilograms ~measured in meters per second #### It is a ratio of two forces.}
::PHY-U1-L5-Q11::Friction on a sliding object points {=opposite the relative sliding motion ~in the direction of velocity in every case ~perpendicular to the surface ~toward the center of Earth #### Kinetic friction opposes relative surface motion.}
::PHY-U1-L5-Q12::For the same tires and speed, a larger coefficient of friction generally allows {=a shorter stopping distance ~a longer stopping distance ~no braking force ~a larger mass #### Larger friction can provide greater deceleration.}
::PHY-U1-L5-Q13::The buoyant force on a submerged object is exerted by {=the surrounding fluid ~Earth only ~the object's inertia ~a rope only #### Pressure differences in the fluid create buoyancy.}
::PHY-U1-L5-Q14::An object floats at rest when {=buoyant force equals weight ~buoyant force is zero ~weight is greater than buoyant force ~no forces act #### Floating equilibrium has zero net vertical force.}
::PHY-U1-L5-Q15::Centripetal force is directed {=toward the center of the circular path ~tangent to the path ~away from the center ~opposite the object's velocity #### Centripetal means center-seeking.}
::PHY-U1-L5-Q16::An object moving in a circle at constant speed is accelerating because {=its velocity direction changes ~its mass changes ~its speed must increase ~all forces are balanced #### Velocity is a vector, so changing direction is acceleration.}
::PHY-U1-L5-Q17::A 2 kg object moves at 3 m/s in a circle of radius 1.5 m. The centripetal-force magnitude is {=12 N ~4 N ~9 N ~18 N #### Fc=mv²/r=2(9)/1.5=12 N.}
::PHY-U1-L5-Q18::Which force can supply centripetal force for a car turning on a flat road? {=static friction ~buoyancy ~spring force only ~weight alone #### Tire-road static friction points inward.}
::PHY-U1-L5-Q19::Which statement about centripetal force is correct? {=It is the inward net force supplied by one or more interactions. ~It is an additional outward force. ~It exists only for planets. ~It always equals weight. #### Centripetal force names a net-force role, not a separate interaction.}
::PHY-U1-L5-Q20::A free-body diagram for a book resting on a table should show {=weight downward and normal force upward ~only weight ~only normal force ~a centripetal force #### The two vertical forces balance.}
::PHY-U1-L5-Q21::A block is pulled right and slides right across a floor. Its free-body diagram includes friction {=to the left ~to the right ~upward ~downward #### Friction opposes the relative sliding.}
::PHY-U1-L5-Q22::For a sled pulled upward at an angle, the pull's upward component usually makes the normal force {=smaller than the weight ~larger than the weight ~equal to zero in every case ~equal to the pull #### The upward component partly supports the sled.}
::PHY-U1-L5-Q23::A spring-mass system is at equilibrium. Which relation is correct? {=The upward spring force equals the downward weight. ~The spring force is zero. ~The mass has a nonzero net force. ~The spring constant equals the mass. #### Equilibrium requires zero net force.}
::PHY-U1-L5-Q24::Which evidence best supports Hooke's law for an ideal spring? {=A straight-line graph of force versus displacement through the origin ~A circular force-displacement graph ~A constant displacement for all forces ~An unlabeled sketch #### Linear proportionality is the predicted pattern.}
::PHY-U1-L5-Q25::A student says, “The normal force always equals weight.” Which response is best? {=It can differ when other vertical forces act or when the surface is inclined. ~It is correct for every situation. ~Normal force is always zero. ~Normal force depends only on speed. #### Normal force is set by the surface interaction and vertical/perpendicular force balance.}

